Question ID: ALG1_U02_L01_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Visual reference: balance both sides; use the inverse operation to keep the equation equivalent.
Solve x + 6 = 14.
{
~A. x = 20#This adds 6 instead of subtracting 6.
=B. x = 8#Correct. Subtract 6 from both sides.
~C. x = -8#The sign does not make the original equation true.
~D. x = 6#This repeats the number added to x.
}

::ALG1_U02_L01_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U02_L01_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Visual reference: balance both sides; use the inverse operation to keep the equation equivalent.
Solve m - 9 = 4.
{
~A. m = -5#This subtracts 9 from 4 instead of adding 9.
~B. m = 9#This is the number subtracted from m.
~C. m = 4#This repeats the right side without isolating m.
=D. m = 13#Correct. Add 9 to both sides.
}

::ALG1_U02_L01_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U02_L01_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Visual reference: balance both sides; use the inverse operation to keep the equation equivalent.
Solve 5n = 35.
{
=A. n = 7#Correct. Divide both sides by 5.
~B. n = 30#This subtracts 5 instead of dividing by 5.
~C. n = 40#This adds 5 instead of undoing multiplication.
~D. n = 175#This multiplies by 5 instead of dividing by 5.
}

::ALG1_U02_L01_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U02_L01_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Visual reference: balance both sides; use the inverse operation to keep the equation equivalent.
Solve p/4 = 7.
{
~A. p = 3#This subtracts 4 from 7.
~B. p = 11#This adds 4, but p/4 = 7 means p is divided by 4, so multiply both sides by 4.
=C. p = 28#Correct. Multiply both sides by 4.
~D. p = 7/4#This divides again instead of undoing division.
}

::ALG1_U02_L01_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U02_L01_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Visual reference: balance both sides; use the inverse operation to keep the equation equivalent.
Why should you check a solution in the original equation?
{
~A. To make the answer larger#Checking verifies an answer; it does not change the value.
=B. To verify that the value makes both sides equal#Correct. A solution must make the original equation true.
~C. To avoid using inverse operations#Inverse operations are still needed to solve.
~D. To remove the variable from the course#Checking is about accuracy, not removing variables from future lessons.
}

